{"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"lyric"},{"kind":"text","text":": ["},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLLyric","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL8MXLLyricV","text":"MXLLyric"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Maps to nested MusicXML "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"<lyric>"},{"type":"text","text":" elements."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"text":"The lyrics associated with this note.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote\/lyric"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote"]]},"sections":[],"metadata":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"lyric"},{"kind":"text","text":": ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL8MXLLyricV","text":"MXLLyric"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}],"role":"symbol","symbolKind":"property","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"roleHeading":"Instance Property","externalID":"s:7IvorMXL7MXLNoteV5lyricSayAA8MXLLyricVGvp","title":"lyric"},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote\/lyric","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"kind":"symbol","references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNote":{"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote","title":"MXLNote","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNote","kind":"identifier"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLNote","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Notes are the most common type of MusicXML data. The MusicXML format distinguishes between elements used for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"sound information and elements used for notation information (e.g., tie is used for sound, tied for notation)."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Thus grace notes do not have a duration element. Cue notes have a duration element, as do forward elements,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"but no tie elements. Having these two types of information available can make interchange easier, as some","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"programs handle one type of information more readily than the other."}],"type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNote/lyric":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnote\/lyric","role":"symbol","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNote\/lyric","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The lyrics associated with this note."}],"title":"lyric","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"lyric","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": [","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:7IvorMXL8MXLLyricV","text":"MXLLyric","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"]","kind":"text"}],"kind":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLLyric":{"type":"topic","title":"MXLLyric","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLLyric"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxllyric","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLLyric","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLLyric"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The lyric type represents text underlays for lyrics. Two text elements that are not separated by an elision"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"element are part of the same syllable, but may have different text formatting. The MusicXML XSD is more strict"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"than the DTD in enforcing this by disallowing a second syllabic element unless preceded by an elision element."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The lyric number indicates multiple lines, though a name can be used as well. Common name examples are verse"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and chorus."}]}}}